# Slice 297 probe (CALLABLE-PLACE-NAMED-RECEPTION, a typed value; Codex 294 review: a typed result is never recoloured as a
# literal, the reachable converters stay): the place's formals are int x, char y; the fn written into it has int y, char x.
# `Places\f(x: n; y: 7)` with `int: n 5`: the int 5 at the selected char x -- the program defines the int -> char receiver
# (convert.lm2: int char narrow lm_stg_convert_int_char), which adds 1.  Exit: the value seen -- 67 (received through the
# receiver: 6 * 10 + 7), 25 for the implicit `convert`.
fn: lm_stg_convert_int_char (int: n) char
    return: (cast: (char) (n + 1))
end: lm_stg_convert_int_char
fn: g (int: y; char: x) int
    return: (cast: (int) x) * 10 + y
end: g
Places:
    fn: f (int: x; char: y) int
        return: x * 10 + (cast: (int) y)
    end: f
end: Places
int: Hw 0
fn: write_place () int
    ---
        @: Places\f Hw
        return: 1
        catch: implements ()
            return: 24
    ---
end: write_place
fn: call_typed () int
    int: n 5
    ---
        int: got Places\f(x: n; y: 7)
        return: got
        catch: convert ()
            return: 25
    ---
end: call_typed
fn: run () int
    @: Hw g g
    if: write_place() != 1
        return: 34
    ---
    return: call_typed()
end: run
int: code run()
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: code; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
